## 8. Commit Quality (High Standards)

**Rule:** Commits are descriptive, atomic, and follow conventions.

**Format:**
```
<type>: <short summary>

<optional detailed description>
```

**Types:**
- `feat:` New feature
- `fix:` Bug fix
- `docs:` Documentation only
- `refactor:` Code restructure (no behavior change)
- `test:` Add/update tests
- `chore:` Tooling, deps, config

**Examples:**
- ✅ `feat: add LSS Shepherding schedule automation`
- ✅ `fix: enforce CW Tuesday-only rule per Event Standards v1`
- ✅ `docs: update AGENTS.md with QA D checklist`
- ❌ `wip` (not descriptive)
- ❌ `fixed stuff` (vague)

**Atomic Commits:**
- One logical change per commit
- Each commit should build/pass tests
- Easy to review, revert, cherry-pick

---

## 9. No Docs-Only Code Changes (Separation)

**Rule:** Documentation PRs should NOT include application code changes (and vice versa).

**Docs-Only PR:**
- Updates `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules`, `docs/**/*.md`
- Title: `docs: <description>`
- Body: Lists files, no code changes mentioned

**Code PR:**
- Updates `backend/**`, `frontend/**`, configs
- Title: `feat:` or `fix:` etc.
- May include minimal inline code comments

**Why:** Easier review, cleaner history, faster merges.

**Exception:** Inline code comments in a feature PR are OK (they're part of the code change).

